WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.137 --> 00:00:06.120
Narrator: Thick green algae blankets,

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.911
frogs,

00:00:07.911 --> 00:00:10.544
green-stained tiles,

00:00:10.544 --> 00:00:12.210
and mosquito nests.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:13.560
These are just a few things

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.390
that can be found inside a pool

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:17.640
that's been left to sit for a few years

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.080
or even a few days,

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.330
depending on its location.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:24.150
Here's how UK pool engineer Miles Laflin,

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.040
better known as Thep00lguy,

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:30.123
salvages these pools by
giving them a deep clean.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.583
This indoor pool hasn't
been used in six years.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:41.370
Plants started growing inside of it,

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.991
which isn't typical for an indoor pool.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.270
Miles starts by pumping out the water.

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:50.903
Miles: Nice and empty,
but not nice and clean.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.030
Time to get to work.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:55.920
Narrator: He brushes all
the debris into a corner

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.720
and disposes of it in a compost heap.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.022
Miles: The main difference
between cleaning

00:01:01.022 --> 00:01:03.630
an indoor and an outdoor
pool is, normally,

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.914
you don't get any sort of
debris in a indoor pool.

00:01:06.914 --> 00:01:08.790
Obviously, this one had
plants growing in it.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.340
Took about five or six years

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:12.750
for this pool to get like that.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:13.950
But for an outdoor pool,

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:16.170
that would probably
only take maybe a year,

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:18.828
year and a half for it
to get like that state.

00:01:24.558 --> 00:01:27.728
Narrator: He power washes
the walls and the floor.

00:01:28.703 --> 00:01:30.145
Miles: Quickly have a
guess at what picture's

00:01:30.145 --> 00:01:33.990
tiled in on the floor.
I went for a dolphin.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:35.490
Turns out it's a sea lion.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:36.761
That's pretty cool though.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.623
Narrator: Then he uses acid to remove

00:01:39.623 --> 00:01:40.830
the remaining stains

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:42.900
and lets it sit before rinsing it away

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.483
and vacuuming up the last bits.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:52.890
These are chlorine tablets.

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:54.450
They're inserted into a dispenser

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:57.003
that slowly feeds chlorine into the pool.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:02.908
Miles adds chlorine granules

00:02:02.908 --> 00:02:05.583
directly into the pool
for an instant release.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:13.320
This blue liquid is algaecide,

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.050
which prevents future algae growth.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.340
Miles: Well, some people think it's me

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.248
adding color to the pool. [laughs]

00:02:20.248 --> 00:02:22.020
"Is that what makes the pool look blue?"

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.681
is what some people message me.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:30.210
Narrator: The jelly
cubes remove phosphates

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:32.040
and small particles from the pool

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.320
and improve chemical efficiency.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.720
This makes the water clearer.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:38.258
Then he adds salt.

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:42.870
It takes a week for the
salt to turn into chlorine

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:45.123
through a process called electrolysis.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:50.727
Miles: This was all within
maybe half a day's work.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:53.992
Narrator: This pool has
only gone a few days

00:02:53.992 --> 00:02:56.400
without chlorine. It's in a greenhouse,

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.340
so it gets hot easily.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:01.800
This allows the algae
to attach to the tiles,

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.248
which are actually blue underneath.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.598
Miles sprays diluted hydrochloric acid

00:03:07.598 --> 00:03:09.006
over the tiles.

00:03:09.006 --> 00:03:12.030
It's just aggressive
enough to detach the algae.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.420
When it starts bubbling up,

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.850
it's a good indication that it's working.

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:20.880
Then he brushes it in

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.147
to disturb the surface of the algae.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:33.935
He hoses off all this and
gives it a good rinse.

00:03:33.935 --> 00:03:36.699
This only takes about 15 minutes.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.075
This pool has a thick
blanket of algae over it.

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:54.930
Miles starts pumping out the water

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.253
with a submersible pump.

00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:59.427
Then he shovels the algae and debris

00:03:59.427 --> 00:04:01.263
out into buckets.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:04.140
Algae buildup like this happens

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:06.671
after a pool is left without chlorine.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:11.077
Miles: When we had the pool empty,

00:04:11.077 --> 00:04:13.061
we realized how many frogs there were.

00:04:13.061 --> 00:04:14.594
I'd never seen so many frogs

00:04:14.594 --> 00:04:16.502
in a pool before in my life.

00:04:16.502 --> 00:04:18.420
Like, every scoop, every shovelful,

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.120
there was maybe one or two frogs on it.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.560
So we had to get them out,

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:23.460
put them on the side of the pool,

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:25.310
and let them run off into the bushes.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.140
Narrator: Miles applies
diluted hydrochloric acid

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.329
to break down the remaining bits.

00:04:33.329 --> 00:04:35.389
Miles: Get out the old Karcher K4.

00:04:35.389 --> 00:04:37.980
Narrator: And it's all power washed away.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.438
Miles: It's all coming up pretty well.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:48.253
Narrator: He breaks up the
hard debris in the drain

00:04:48.253 --> 00:04:50.736
with a screwdriver and vacuums it out.

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:05.220
The dirtiest part of
the pool is the filters,

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:07.570
which catch the debris
and continuously circulate

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:09.776
the water to keep the pool clean.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:15.630
These filters get the job done with sand,

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:18.647
so he adds five bags of
sand to replenish them.

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:29.347
He also adds chlorine and salt

00:05:29.347 --> 00:05:32.133
directly into the pool
to balance the chemicals.

00:05:33.508 --> 00:05:35.910
Miles: A few bags of salt needed.

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:37.653
Three 25-kilo bags.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:47.641
Narrator: He uses a brush to help

00:05:47.641 --> 00:05:49.508
dissolve them into the water.

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:54.453
Miles: And that's that job done.

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:06.180
Narrator: This pool has
been sitting for one summer,

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:07.500
which is just enough time

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:09.785
for mosquitoes to colonize it.

00:06:09.785 --> 00:06:11.472
Miles: Literally so bad I had to, like,

00:06:11.472 --> 00:06:12.867
run away from the pool.

00:06:12.867 --> 00:06:14.220
Narrator: The customer has requested

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:16.890
for the pool to be cleaned
without emptying it.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.710
Miles starts by adding chlorine granules.

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:21.300
He also shocks the pool,

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:24.090
which kills off the
algae and contaminants.

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:26.460
The process causes all
the small debris particles

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:29.310
to clump together and become heavy.

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:30.720
After a few days,

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:32.820
the debris sinks to
the bottom of the pool,

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:35.610
and the water is crystal clear.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:37.783
He vacuums out the debris from the bottom

00:06:37.783 --> 00:06:40.276
and finishes by adding more chlorine.

